<reference_puzzle index="1" source="train" puzzle_id="15660dd6" lines="33-54">
<description>
Combine each column's repeated template mask with its single solid color swatch.

Input structure:
- The grid is a table of framed panels separated by background rows/columns.
- The leftmost column gives one solid row-label color for each panel row.
- In each panel column, exactly one panel has a solid non-background interior,
  while the other panels share the same mask drawn in one template color on
  the background.

Rule:
- For every panel column, find the row containing the solid interior swatch.
- Rebuild one output panel using that row's left label as the frame color.
- Fill the repeated template mask with the swatch color and leave the mask
  background cells as background; separate rebuilt panels with one
  background column.

Key insight:
The table encodes a colorized stencil per column: the solid panel supplies
the paint color and frame label, while the duplicate template panels supply
the shape mask.
</description>
</reference_puzzle>

<reference_puzzle index="2" source="train" puzzle_id="c9680e90" lines="52-67">
<description>
Move each mirrored bottom source pixel to the far end of its trail and redraw the top half as its reflection.

Input structure
- A solid non-background row separates equal-height top and bottom halves.
- The top half has one marker color on the background.
- In the bottom half, exactly one color has positions that mirror the top markers across the separator, and the other color forms trails touching those source pixels.

Rule
- Identify the bottom source color by reflecting each bottom color into the top half and matching the top marker set.
- For every bottom source pixel, follow its unique 4-connected trail of the other bottom color to the trail endpoint.
- Clear the old top markers, bottom sources, and trails; place the source color at each endpoint and place the top marker color at the reflected endpoint.

Key insight
- The top half is only a mirror display of the bottom moving sources; the actual motion is encoded by the trail attached to each bottom source pixel.
</description>
</reference_puzzle>

<reference_puzzle index="3" source="train" puzzle_id="af726779" lines="17-35">
<description>
Grow an alternating pink/orange triangular trace downward from the horizontal orange seed row.

Input structure:
- The grid has a green background and one horizontal row containing all orange foreground pixels.
- The seed row may contain isolated orange pixels and adjacent orange runs.
- Pink is absent from the input and is used for the first generated row.

Rule:
- Copy the input grid.
- Two rows below the current active row, place a child pixel between each active pair
  whose middle cell in the current row is background.
- Alternate generated row colors between pink and orange, stopping when no children
  can be generated or the next row would leave the grid.

Key insight:
- Only an active-background-active triplet in the current row reproduces; contiguous
  active runs do not produce children through occupied middle cells.
</description>
</reference_puzzle>

<reference_puzzle index="4" source="eval" puzzle_id="142ca369" lines="6-19">
<description>
Trace diagonal rays from triangular arrows, reflecting off colored wall pixels.

Input structure:
- bg grid with monochrome 3-pixel L-shaped "arrows" (2x2 bbox missing one corner) and scattered colored wall pixels.

Rule:
- Each arrow fires a ray diagonally away from its missing corner, painting bg cells in its color.
- A head-on wall hit reverses both axes; a side-touch wall hit flips the touched axis only.
- On any bounce the ray adopts the wall's color and recolors the contact cell.
- Ray stops when it exits the grid or reaches another arrow.

Key insight: Two distinct reflection types (corner vs side-touch) plus color inheritance at each bounce drive the path geometry.
</description>
</reference_puzzle>

<reference_puzzle index="5" source="train" puzzle_id="c803e39c" lines="31-40">
<description>
Expand the second mask into a block grid by stamping the first mask with the two payload colors.

Input structure
- A background grid has four panels separated by three full-height separator columns.
- The first two panels contain same-size binary masks; the last two contain solid same-size color squares.
Rule
- Use the second mask as an outer layout. For each foreground cell in that outer mask, place a tile equal to the first mask, coloring its foreground with the first solid-square color and its background with the second solid-square color; outer background cells become all second solid-square color.
Key insight
- The output is the Kronecker product of the two binary masks, with the two right-hand panel colors providing the palette.
</description>
</reference_puzzle>

<reference_puzzle index="6" source="train" puzzle_id="e3fe1151" lines="36-55">
<description>
Fill the one blank in each corner region so all four regions have the same color multiset.

Input structure:
- The dominant background color forms one solid separator row and one solid
  separator column.
- The separator cross splits the grid into four equal rectangular corner regions.
- Each corner region contains exactly one background-colored blank pixel.

Rule:
- Count the non-background colors in each corner region.
- The completed region multiset is defined by the maximum observed count of
  each color across the four regions.
- Replace each region's blank pixel with the single color needed to match that
  completed multiset.

Key insight:
- The separator color inside a corner region is a missing value marker; comparing
  the four regions reveals the shared multiset without using numeric color order.
</description>
</reference_puzzle>

<reference_puzzle index="7" source="eval" puzzle_id="4c416de3" lines="99-118">
<description>
Replicate a pre-drawn corner pattern to every other marker-bearing corner.

Input structure:
- bg = most frequent color. Hollow 0-frames (possibly partial L-shapes); each
  frame-corner cell is either 0 or a painted (non-bg, non-0) color.
- >= 1 'source' corner: the pattern is already drawn (one color, both outward
  cells and an inward-diagonal trail).
- Other corners: a single colored pixel on the inward diagonal at the pattern's
  deepest (k,k) offset; the pixel's color is that corner's target color.

Rule:
- Extract the canonical (TL-oriented) pattern from any source.
- For each non-source corner whose marker cell is colored, paint the same
  pattern rotated to that corner's orientation, using the marker's color.

Key insight: the pattern is symmetric about the corner's inward-diagonal axis
(canonical dr == dc), so it reflects cleanly across all four corner orientations.
Painting uses each target's OWN marker color, not the source color.
</description>
</reference_puzzle>

<reference_puzzle index="8" source="eval" puzzle_id="de809cff" lines="86-103">
<description>
Turn black dents in two colored regions into marked opposite-color patches.

Input structure:
- Black background with two large foreground colors.
- The large regions contain small black dents and a few one-cell color specks
  or protrusions along region boundaries.

Rule:
- Locally clean the two foreground regions: isolated specks become black, and
  one-cell protrusions surrounded by the other region take that region's color.
- Each original black dent whose cleaned 3x3 neighborhood is mostly one
  foreground color becomes the center of a 3x3 patch in the opposite color.
- The dent center itself is recolored teal (8).

Key insight: The teal pixels mark the original black defects; the surrounding
3x3 patch uses the other foreground color, after first removing boundary noise.
</description>
</reference_puzzle>

<reference_puzzle index="9" source="train" puzzle_id="d255d7a7" lines="99-114">
<description>
Move the patterned edge band to the opposite edge along every row or column whose band slice contains the trail color.

Input structure
- The dominant color is the background.
- The two foreground colors are a frequent trail color and a rarer marker color.
- All marker pixels lie nearest to one grid edge, and that edge contains trail-color runs that define the source-band thickness.

Rule
- Choose the source edge identified by the marker pixels.
- A row or column perpendicular to that edge moves if its source-band slice contains the trail color.
- Copy that slice unchanged to the opposite edge, erase the remaining trail cells to background, and keep marker-only lines fixed.

Key insight
- The marker color identifies the active edge, while the trail color identifies which parallel rows or columns carry an edge-band slice across the grid.
</description>
</reference_puzzle>

<reference_puzzle index="10" source="eval" puzzle_id="2c181942" lines="7-21">
<description>
Attach monochrome sprites to the matching sides of a central hollow square.

Input structure:
- bg = background. A hollow NxN square (hub) with bg corners/interior and four differently-colored edges (top, bottom, left, right).
- 2-4 monochrome sprites scattered on the grid, each matching one hub edge color.

Rule:
- For each hub edge color, find the matching sprite outside the hub.
- Rotate the sprite (0/90/180/270°) so its contact edge has exactly N-2 contiguous cells aligned with the hub side, and the rotated sprite is mirror-symmetric along the contact-edge axis (UD for left/right, LR for top/bottom).
- Translate it to adjoin that hub side. 
- Clear everything else to bg; output is hub + attached sprites only.

Key insight: Each edge color uniquely pairs a sprite to a side; symmetry along the contact-edge axis disambiguates the correct rotation.
</description>
</reference_puzzle>

